Subject: [PATCH 4/3] arm64-asm: include <assert.h>

Not part of the posted series -- ours, and required to BUILD it.

arm64-asm.c calls assert(0) at two sites (asm_fp_binary_opcode and
asm_memory_opcode) without including <assert.h>. Whether that breaks depends
entirely on how tcc is being built:

  cross targets   tcc.c is compiled as ONE translation unit and something in
                  that include chain already pulls in <assert.h>, so assert
                  expands to its macro and resolves to __assert_fail. Builds
                  and links fine -- which is how this went unnoticed.

  native build    arm64-asm.c is compiled SEPARATELY into libtcc.a. No macro,
                  so `assert(0)` is an implicit function declaration and the
                  link fails:

                      libtcc.a(arm64-asm.o): in function `asm_opcode':
                      arm64-asm.c: undefined reference to `assert'

The native path is the one that matters here: it is what a native aarch64
build does, and it is what tcc-userland-arm64 runs.

Verified by configuring with --cpu=arm64 (which selects the libtcc.a
structure), reproducing the exact link error, applying this, and getting a
working tcc that still assembles all 16 of musl 1.2.5's aarch64 asm files.
---
 arm64-asm.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/arm64-asm.c b/arm64-asm.c
--- a/arm64-asm.c
+++ b/arm64-asm.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 /*************************************************************/
 #define USING_GLOBALS
 #include "tcc.h"
+#include <assert.h>
 
 #define OPT_REG    (1U << 0)
 #define OPT_WREG   (1U << 1)
